WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.700
All right how about this, for something to talk about:
the morality of Shadow of the Colossus

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:16.980
Shadow of the Colossus was originally released for the Playstation 2 in 2005

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:21.340
and re-released in high definition for the Playstation 3 in 2011.

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:28.080
Serving as a loose prequel to 2001's Ico, the game places the player in control of Wander, a young man

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:33.060
who has brought the body of a young woman named Mono to a temple in the far off Forbidden Lands,

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:36.440
to petition an entity known as Dormin to resurrect her.

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:42.640
Dormin agrees to Wander's request, but first, tasks Wander with destroying the 16 Colossi that hold

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:49.400
Dormin captive, and the rest of the game unfolds from there, as the player first locates the various Colossi,

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:52.940
figures out their weakness, and slays them.

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:58.520
Shadow of the Colossus has rightly received praise from many

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:01.900
for its emotional complexity and moral nuance.

00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:06.240
Now, it's important to remember going into this that, as with all narratives,

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:10.100
our interests are instinctively aligned to those of the protagonist.

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:14.680
Even when those goals are bad or evil, we are emotionally predisposed

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:18.300
to share the protagonist's desire to achieve those goals.

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:23.580
So much of what we will focus on, are the counter-implications to the otherwise self-evident

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:26.820
emotional value of fulfilling Wander's quest.

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:32.660
Wander's goal is one that is entirely congruous with typical fantasy video game narratives,

00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:37.080
Essentially, it's just a remix of slaying the dragon and rescuing the princess.

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:40.160
Slay the Colossus and resurrect the doomed girl.

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:42.480
But this quest is tinged with uncertainty.

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:48.360
Wander's superficially virtuous goal, leading him to do things that are... questionable

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:54.240
The 16 guardians of Dormin's power are hulking monsters of earth, fur and masonry,

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:57.060
with a design aesthetic evocative of architecture.

00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:03.660
A palette of materials and shapes that makes them look like some ancient forgotten watchtower come to life.

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:09.460
They are fearsome, imposing and often wrought on a scale that makes them difficult to comprehend.

00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:12.360
While Wander must mount or ride all of the Colossi

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:16.240
in order to slay them by attacking their literally magical weak point,

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:23.620
most still fit into something approaching a human scale, being the size of a large animal or small house.

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:28.280
But a few are so large that they become terrain.

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:39.240
The game presents the Colossi in conflicting emotional context, of fear, and empathy.

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:42.660
The first of these contexts, fear is the easy one.

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:47.300
Given their scale, their alien composition, their seeming indestructibility,

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:53.820
it is easy to frame the Colossi as opponents, to present them as adversaries to be bested in a contest of skill,

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:58.900
to show them as a hostile opposition to your protagonist-centered-righteousness.

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:04.800
They are large, they wield weapons of massive proportions, their silhouettes are frightening and jagged,

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:07.040
and many of them are outright deadly.

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:11.460
However with each one, the designers have carefully crafted a story

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:14.820
that begins here with fear and intimidation,

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:18.660
and progresses towards empathy and sadness.

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:22.180
More often than not this is a story told literally from bottom to top

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:27.920
as the most common pattern for engaging with the various Colossi is to approach from the ground,

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:33.240
find a way to climb onto the Colossus and then work your way to the head for the killing blow.

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:37.980
The story of each Colossus then is told from the feet to the face.

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:42.620
Armored hooves, stone spikes, and wrought-iron give way to leathery skin

00:03:42.620 --> 00:03:45.580
and fur that, although just a digital simulacra,

00:03:45.580 --> 00:03:52.140
one can't help but imagine as bristly and not altogether unpleasant like a camel or a bear.

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:56.120
From there the player directs Wander to the head where the camera swings around

00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:59.120
to frame Wander along with the Colossus' face.

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:03.760
The eyes which looked small, beady, and mildly sinister from the ground,

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:07.620
here look plaintive, curious, almost playful.

00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:12.420
Up-close, many of the Colossi have faces that are more than a little silly and childlike

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:15.060
A favorite of mine being the monkey-like Gaius.

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:19.800
From the ground, Gaius is a menacing thing, an unstable knight on ball feet,

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:25.740
that tries to crush you with its massive stone sword, until you climb to its round silly face

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:29.000
and are expected to plunge your own sword into its head.

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:34.720
The game however doesn't rub this in.

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:39.640
There is no grand moment of pathos– a single-tear-in-the-rain shot,

00:04:39.640 --> 00:04:43.640
there is only your own self-doubt in the correctness of Dormin's quest,

00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:51.460
your own sense of right and wrong debating if it's the right choice to destroy something as majestic as Avion.

00:04:51.460 --> 00:04:57.200
Part of how the game sells this complexity is by not simply playing the same notes over and over.

00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:02.540
This is to keep the gameplay feeling fresh, keeping the puzzles from getting too repetitive and formulaic,

00:05:02.540 --> 00:05:06.160
but it also serves to effectively muddy the moral waters.

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:12.800
Some of the Colossi are simply horrifying in their design, unlovable bodies of menace and blades.

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:17.080
The tortoise Basaran is a literal ball of spikes and is highly aggressive,

00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:19.620
sieging Wander with its projectile attacks.

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:24.960
Basaran is as barren and unlovable as the blasted wasteland it lives in.

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:30.000
The sky serpent Phalanx is impressive, daunting, but also utterly alien.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:34.360
The water-bound Pelagia has a crown of teeth ringing its head.

00:05:34.360 --> 00:05:38.620
The three smallest Colossi are also three of the most aggressive and deadly

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:45.360
Two of them–Celosia and Cenobia–call on primal fears, their design evoking a pastiche of lions and wolves,

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:47.060
ancient human predators,

00:05:47.060 --> 00:05:51.020
however, even then there are moments of humanity injected.

00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:55.700
Celosia fears the fire and recoils when Wander wields a torch in its face,

00:05:55.700 --> 00:06:00.940
driving it backwards until it falls from a cliff shattering its armor and revealing its weak spots.

00:06:00.940 --> 00:06:06.340
Similarly the fight against the terrifying skeletal horse Phaedra is punctuated by a moment

00:06:06.340 --> 00:06:10.900
where Wander must stab Phaedra in the back of the neck to make it seized up in pain

00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:14.060
so he can deliver the killing blow to its head.

00:06:14.060 --> 00:06:18.160
All of these storytelling mechanisms are brought to a climax in the ultimate fight

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:20.640
against the 16th Colossus Malus.

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:27.020
Setting the stage for the encounter,

00:06:27.020 --> 00:06:28.700
Wander must travel to the far South,

00:06:28.700 --> 00:06:32.440
leaping across a rickety bridge on the back of his horse Agro.

00:06:32.440 --> 00:06:38.300
The bridge collapses, Wander is thrown from Agro's back and Agro plummets into the ravine.

00:06:38.300 --> 00:06:40.300
Deprived of his last living companion,

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:44.660
Wander must ascend to the top of the plateau to face his final challenge.

00:06:46.600 --> 00:06:50.160
Malus is enormous.
Towering over the southern plateau,

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:53.280
Malus is the tallest of the Colossi by almost double.

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:55.240
It is also the deadliest of them all,

00:06:55.240 --> 00:07:00.040
firing bolts of energy that home in on Wander sending him skittering across the ground.

00:07:00.040 --> 00:07:06.060
The arena in which you engage Malus is a battlefield, littered with barricades and trenches,

00:07:06.060 --> 00:07:11.880
almost as though whatever ancient people built Malus found themselves laid siege by their own creation

00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:15.440
until they were able to bolt the Colossus' feet to the ground

00:07:15.440 --> 00:07:19.580
leaving it abandoned and stationary, at the edge of the world.

00:07:19.580 --> 00:07:22.800
Approaching Malus feels like stepping into Armageddon.

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:27.300
A rain-soaked plateau still scarred from some ancient battle.

00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:30.600
However even through all that, as Wander nears the end of the puzzle,

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:36.660
he is brought face-to-face with the final Colossus where he is inspected like an oddity.

00:07:36.660 --> 00:07:41.860
Malus' eyes switch from the aggressive orange high alert state to the passive teal.

00:07:41.860 --> 00:07:45.780
Even this, the final moments, there is pause.

00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:49.600
The approach to the final sigil could just as easily have been framed and executed

00:07:49.600 --> 00:07:51.760
as it so often is in the god of war series.

00:07:51.760 --> 00:07:55.320
Another set of games from the same era concerned with the main character

00:07:55.320 --> 00:07:59.120
crawling on gigantic opponents in order to slay them.

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:02.700
But no.
This was not a technological limitation.

00:08:02.700 --> 00:08:07.600
The game is already full of so many impressive moments of transition that this pause,

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:13.560
where Wonder stands and is  inspected by the being he will soon kill, is a conscious decision.

00:08:13.560 --> 00:08:18.500
They had the power to do almost anything they imagined and they chose this.

00:08:18.500 --> 00:08:21.520
Wander is tasked by Dormin with slaying 16 beings,

00:08:21.520 --> 00:08:27.780
none of which truly bear any ill will towards Wander even as he poses a threat to their very existence.

00:08:27.780 --> 00:08:33.860
Beings who aren't mere automatons, but inhabit some space in between construct and living thing.

00:08:33.860 --> 00:08:38.520
They are stone and metal animated by magic but also flesh and fur

00:08:38.520 --> 00:08:41.960
and they cry out in pain when Wander plunges his sword into them.

00:08:41.960 --> 00:08:45.260
Critical to building the complexity of the game's emotional landscape,

00:08:45.260 --> 00:08:48.560
is the composition of the end of each Colossus' life.

00:08:48.560 --> 00:08:52.680
As Wander climbs each Colossus the music swells with heroic energy,

00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:57.380
the kind of blood-pumping soundtracks you would expect to accompany great deeds.

00:08:57.380 --> 00:09:02.260
But, with that final blow, the music turns sad, grieving.

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:12.240
Wander has his moment of heroic victory as the Colossus crumbles around him,

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:16.720
before the black tendrils of Dormin's power escape, and surge into his body,

00:09:16.720 --> 00:09:19.140
leaving him unconscious on the ground.

00:09:19.140 --> 00:09:25.160
The anthropomorphization of the Colossi, the injection of human qualities to evoke empathy,

00:09:25.160 --> 00:09:27.920
is played out in reverse on Wander.

00:09:27.920 --> 00:09:31.660
With each Colossus he destroys, his physical form degrades,

00:09:31.660 --> 00:09:36.900
his humanity withering away until he's left a husk to be possessed by Dormin.

00:09:36.900 --> 00:09:41.820
It is not built to guilt the player but it is still made to feel tragic.

00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:44.520
The game makes no commentary on choice, however,

00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:49.600
because at the end of the day, while Shadow of the Colossus is a participatory experience,

00:09:49.600 --> 00:09:52.580
it is not about that participation.

00:09:52.580 --> 00:09:56.640
Shadow of the Colossus is definitively Wander's story.

00:09:56.640 --> 00:10:01.100
Even though the game world is open with almost the entire area accessible from the very start,

00:10:01.100 --> 00:10:05.580
the player is not given the opportunity to alter Wander's ultimate path,

00:10:05.580 --> 00:10:11.820
Nor is the player impelled with the notion that they can make a different choice, perhaps even the right choice,

00:10:11.820 --> 00:10:15.040
by turning off the game or putting the controller down.

00:10:15.040 --> 00:10:19.400
The player's participation is not in complicity but in reenactment,

00:10:19.400 --> 00:10:23.100
the story projected as myth, the game as retelling.

00:10:23.100 --> 00:10:27.660
The player ultimately inhabits the role of Wander in the same way an actor becomes Macbeth

00:10:27.660 --> 00:10:30.160
without assuming the guilt for Duncan's murder.

00:10:30.160 --> 00:10:36.540
Like a performer, the player experiences the game simultaneously as participant and observer,

00:10:36.540 --> 00:10:42.360
aware of the external context of the story even as their participation makes it real.

00:10:42.360 --> 00:10:45.620
The brilliance of Shadow of the Colossus' emotional landscape

00:10:45.620 --> 00:10:49.220
doesn't truly come together until after the final showdown.

00:10:49.220 --> 00:10:53.920
Throughout Wander's journey, he is pursued by the shaman Emon and his entourage of soldiers,

00:10:53.920 --> 00:10:59.200
who finally arrive in the Forbidden Land just in time to witness Malus' idol in the temple

00:10:59.200 --> 00:11:01.300
shattering with Malus' death.

00:11:01.300 --> 00:11:03.480
There is a standoff between Wander and Emon

00:11:03.480 --> 00:11:07.280
that results in Wander's body being fully possessed by Dormin,

00:11:07.280 --> 00:11:11.880
though only the masculine component of Dormin's voice remains.

00:11:11.880 --> 00:11:18.540
Emon flees, casting a powerful binding spell that traps Dormin long enough for Emon and the surviving soldiers to escape,

00:11:18.540 --> 00:11:21.940
collapsing  the bridge out of the Forbidden Land behind them.

00:11:21.940 --> 00:11:25.300
Dormin recedes, leaving Wander, his body broken and frail,

00:11:25.300 --> 00:11:31.300
to resist the spell's pull, but in the end it is irresistible and he is pulled into the void.

00:11:31.300 --> 00:11:34.540
When the dust has finally settled Mono, the dead girl,

00:11:34.540 --> 00:11:38.340
wakes up from the altar as a wounded Agro limps into the temple.

00:11:38.340 --> 00:11:42.420
Together they inspect the pool where the Dormin-possessed Wander was entombed

00:11:42.420 --> 00:11:46.360
and find an infant boy with curious horns growing from his head,

00:11:46.360 --> 00:11:51.400
aft-shadowing the titular main character of the team's previous game Ico.

00:11:51.400 --> 00:11:55.860
The three of them silently wind their way together up the temple to a hidden garden.

00:11:55.860 --> 00:12:00.140
Dormin, in all their ferocity and animosity towards the shaman,

00:12:00.140 --> 00:12:05.160
through their manipulation of Wander to their own ends, is still a god of life.

00:12:05.160 --> 00:12:11.080
Dormin is caged because Dormin is troublesome. Dormin complicates the hierarchy.

00:12:11.080 --> 00:12:16.960
Very little of their character is actually demonstrated, and what is demonstrated always exists in duality.

00:12:16.960 --> 00:12:21.480
As shadows gather around Wander, doves gather around Mono.

00:12:21.480 --> 00:12:25.700
What we are told of Dormin comes almost entirely from a proven adversary,

00:12:25.700 --> 00:12:32.040
Lord Emon, a man who was himself willing to ritually sacrifice Mono to unknown ends.

00:12:32.040 --> 00:12:35.440
Wander's actions are tragic, lamentable even,

00:12:35.440 --> 00:12:39.780
but the consequences of those actions are not nearly so clear-cut.

00:12:39.780 --> 00:12:46.340
The final moments with Mono in the garden are framed as peaceful, serene, a moment of hope and rebirth.

00:12:46.340 --> 00:12:50.040
What comes after that, the threads that connect Shadow of the Colossus to Ico,

00:12:50.040 --> 00:12:56.360
are left largely to implication and innuendo, mythical gaps to be filled by speculation.

00:12:56.360 --> 00:13:00.140
But the one thing that is clear is that this is not the end.

00:13:00.140 --> 00:13:02.880
Dormin's release does not trigger Armageddon.

00:13:02.880 --> 00:13:07.880
By the time of Ico, things appear to be in disrepair but those events, that world,

00:13:07.880 --> 00:13:13.000
is separated from Mono in the garden by hundreds, perhaps even thousands of years.

00:13:13.000 --> 00:13:20.000
Such is moral complexity, events with upsides and downsides in both the present, future, and far future,

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:27.120
The ultimate judgment of Wander is left to you, the observer, to decide based on your own values.

